A||N||10||Jeneum||Hard starting||||9||
Z||000000||Jeneum||11-30-1999||11:13 PM||||I just got an 86 SE with the V-6 and have tuned it up with plugs wires and fliters and injector cleaner but am still having problems when it comes to starting.  it takes it a little whilt a few turns to start when cold.  Somtimes when it has been running for a while it will fire right up, it just when it's cold.<BR>Any ideas???||||reg||9
Z||000001||Linx||11-30-1999||11:28 PM||||Maybe the cold start injector is bad?  Your engine has a 7th injector; at start, it shoots a quick load of fuel into the air intake for a faster start.  It's a solenoid...maybe it's shot.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 11-30-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||12-01-1999||12:10 AM||||I don't know about the V6, but I'm having problems starting my 88 coupe. It usually starts fine cold, but after the engine heats up, it will crank and crank, usually starting on the second try. I just replaced the fuel filter, fuel pump. Turns out I need a computer! Anyone out there have a computer for an 88 four cylinder automatic?||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||12-01-1999||12:59 AM||||When giving a complete tuneup make sure to change out the coil, module and pick up coil. These can also give you problems during start up.||||reg||7
Z||000004||mwbackus||12-01-1999||09:46 AM||||turbo86se :<BR>The symtoms you describe are usually associated with leaking fuel injectors on the V6.  What are the factors that determined a bad computer in your case (on the L4)?  The reason that I am interested, is that there have been several cases of this on the V6, where the injectors were not responsible, and the actual cause is still unclear.<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in my user profile)<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 12-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||12-01-1999||12:43 PM||||One of the temperature sensors controls the cold start injector.  I can't remember which one right now.  Also see if the cold start injector is leaking fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bill Osmer||12-01-1999||01:20 PM||||Jeneum -<P>I just got through troubleshooting a similar symptom on mine. When I got my 86 GT, it started promptly at the turn of the key. Lately, it would take cranking for several minutes, then would start OK. What I noticed was that I would crank long enough for the oil pressure to come up, then it would start. This indicated that a failed FUEL PUMP RELAY is a likely candidate. I found mine, changed it, and the problem was fixed. BTW, the fuel pump relay comes on just as the key is turned, and only stays on for 2 seconds (for pressurizing the fuel lines). As long as there is fuel pressure (or oil pressure), the relay won't come on again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||12-01-1999||01:23 PM||||My 88 Formula starts easily cold but once heated will crank several tiems and I may need to press the gas pedal to speed it up. I have recently bluprinted my injectors. Could this be a symptom of the cold start allways on? If so why?||||reg||9
Z||000008||Mark||12-01-1999||07:31 PM||||Speaking of cold start injectors, does anyone have a good feel for just how long the cold start injector is energized during a "cold" start?  I would expect that the time would be a function of outside air temperature.  Also, is there a simple test for leakage?||||reg||1
Z||000009||mwbackus||12-01-1999||08:21 PM||||The cold start valve circuit is only energized "while cranking", the thermal switch determines if the valve will activate (or not) while the starter is cranking.<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in user profile)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||12-01-1999||08:35 PM||||I just had similar problems with my 86 GT, and I replaced the pickup coil.  It was hard to start sometimes, and I had replaced the fuel pump relay already.  I had no spark at all when cranking this time, so I popped the dist cap and saw green corrosion all over the connectors and wires where it attaches to the ignition module.  I had never replaced the pickup coil, so I verified the ignition module was OK (free at most parts stores), and bought a pickup coil for 8 bucks.  It's not hard to replace, but you have to take apart the distributor.  My coil apart as I was removing it.  Check out the Fiero On Line Service Guide for the how-to, if you are unfamiliar. ||||reg||1
